# ✅ **1) Binary Operator Overloading ( + ) Using MEMBER FUNCTION**

## 🎯 What it does  
Adds two complex numbers:

```
t3 = t1 + t2;
```

Member function version means:

```
t1.operator+(t2);
```

---

## ⭐ **Simple Code (Member Function)**

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Complex {
    int r, i;

public:
    Complex(int x = 0, int y = 0) {
        r = x;
        i = y;
    }

    // Member function for +
    Complex operator+(Complex c2) {
        Complex temp;
        temp.r = r + c2.r;
        temp.i = i + c2.i;
        return temp;      // return new object
    }

    void display() {
        cout << r << " + " << i << "i\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    Complex t1(5, 4);
    Complex t2(3, 12);

    cout << "First complex number is ";
    t1.display();
    cout << "Second complex number is ";
    t2.display();

    Complex t3;
    t3 = t1 + t2;    // calls operator+

    cout << "Addition of above two complex numbers is ";
    t3.display();

    return 0;
}
```

✔ Output: `8 + 16i`

---

# ✅ **2) Binary Operator Overloading ( + ) Using FRIEND FUNCTION**

## 🎯 Friend version difference  
- Needs **two arguments**  
- Calls:

```
t3 = operator+(t1, t2);
```

---

## ⭐ **Simple Code (Friend Function)**

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Complex {
    int r, i;

public:
    Complex(int x = 0, int y = 0) {
        r = x;
        i = y;
    }

    friend Complex operator+(Complex c1, Complex c2);

    void display() {
        cout << r << " + " << i << "i\n";
    }
};

// Friend function
Complex operator+(Complex c1, Complex c2) {
    Complex temp;
    temp.r = c1.r + c2.r;
    temp.i = c1.i + c2.i;
    return temp;
}

int main() {
    Complex t1(5, 4);
    Complex t2(3, 12);

    Complex t3 = t1 + t2;

    cout << "Addition is ";
    t3.display();

    return 0;
}
```

✔ Same output: `8 + 16i`

