# ✅ **3) Assignment Operator Overloading ( = )**

## 🎯 Why needed?  
Sometimes class has pointers, dynamic memory, or special copying rules.  
Assignment must be overloaded to properly copy values.

---

## ⭐ **EXAM-SIMPLE Assignment Code**

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Sample {
    int d;

public:
    Sample(int x = 0) { d = x; }

    // Assignment operator (must be member function)
    void operator=(Sample s2) {
        d = s2.d;
    }

    void display() {
        cout << d << endl;
    }
};

int main() {
    Sample s3(5);
    Sample s4(6);
    Sample s5;

    cout << "Data of s3: ";
    s3.display();

    cout << "Data of s4: ";
    s4.display();

    s5 = s3 = s4;  // chained assignment

    cout << "Data of s5: ";
    s5.display();

    return 0;
}
```

✔ Output matches your screenshot.

