# 🚀 PART 1 — RUN-TIME POLYMORPHISM (VIRTUAL FUNCTIONS)

## ⭐ What is Run-Time Polymorphism?

When **same function name** exists in **base class** and **derived class**, C++ must decide:

- which version to run **at run time**  
- depending on which object the pointer is pointing to  

This is called:

### ✔ **Dynamic Binding**  
### ✔ **Late Binding**  
### ✔ **Run-Time Polymorphism**

And it happens ONLY with:

### ❗ virtual functions

---

## ⭐ Without virtual → WRONG behavior (Static Binding)

### Example:

```cpp
class A {
public:
    void show() { cout << "Show in A\n"; }
};

class B : public A {
public:
    void show() { cout << "Show in B\n"; }
};
```

Now:

```cpp
A *ptr;
B obj;
ptr = &obj;
ptr->show();    // Which show() is called?
```

👉 Since show() is **NOT virtual**, C++ binds at compile time.  
It sees pointer type = A*, so it calls **A::show()**.

---

## ⭐ With virtual → CORRECT behavior

```cpp
class A {
public:
    virtual void show() { cout << "Show in A\n"; }
};

class B : public A {
public:
    void show() { cout << "Show in B\n"; }
};
```

Now:

```cpp
A *ptr;
B obj;
ptr = &obj;
ptr->show();     // Now shows: "Show in B"
```

✔ Because virtual tells C++:  
"Decide which show() to run **at runtime**, based on object actually pointed to."

---

# 🎯 SIMPLE, CLEAN RUN-TIME POLYMORPHISM CODE (Exam Perfect)

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class A {
public:
    virtual void show() {
        cout << "Show of Base class\n";
    }
};

class B : public A {
public:
    void show() {
        cout << "Show of Derived class\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    A *ptr;      // base class pointer
    B obj;       // derived class object

    ptr = &obj;  // pointer now points to derived object
    ptr->show(); // calls B::show() because of virtual

    return 0;
}
```

✔ Output:  
```
Show of Derived class
```

