# 🔥 FIRST — What is actually happening?

We have:

```
class A { virtual void show(); };
class B : public A { void show(); };
```

Then:

```cpp
A *ptr;
B obj;
ptr = &obj;
ptr->show();
```

Now the big question:

### ❓ Why does ptr->show() call B::show() ?

---

# 🎯 KEY ANSWER
### 👉 Because the pointer is pointing to a **B object**, not an A object.

Even though the pointer’s **type is A***, the **actual object in memory is B**.

C++ sees:

- **Pointer type** = A*  
- **Actual object** = B  

Since `show()` is **virtual**, C++ says:

> “I won’t decide now. At runtime, I will check which object ptr is pointing to.”

At runtime:

```
ptr → B object
```

So C++ calls:

```
B::show()
```

This is run-time polymorphism.

---

# 🎯 SECOND — Why do we use a BASE CLASS pointer?

Because:

### ✔ One pointer type (A*) can point to MANY derived objects (B, C, D…)

Example:

```
class A {};
class B : public A {};
class C : public A {};
class D : public A {};
```

Now you can do:

```cpp
A *ptr;

B b;
C c;
D d;

ptr = &b;
ptr->show();   // B version

ptr = &c;
ptr->show();   // C version

ptr = &d;
ptr->show();   // D version
```

See?  
One pointer can handle ALL derived classes.

This is the entire point of polymorphism (many forms, one interface).

