# 🔥 1) Why Virtual Functions Exist  
**Problem:**  
When a base pointer points to a derived object:

```cpp
A *ptr;
B obj;
ptr = &obj;
ptr->show();  
```

If `show()` is NOT virtual → C++ calls **base class version** (WRONG).

Why?  
Because the compiler decides the function at compile time (static binding).

But we WANT:  
“Call the function of the object actually pointed to.”

That object = **B**  
So function = **B::show()**

To allow this, we use:

### ✔ `virtual`  
Now binding happens at **run time** → dynamic binding → run-time polymorphism.

---

# 🔥 2) Core Definition (Exam Line)

**Virtual function**:  
A function declared in base class using keyword `virtual` so that,  
when accessed through a base pointer, the **derived class version** is executed **at runtime**, based on the type of the object pointed to.

---

# 🔥 3) Basic Virtual Function Example (Your screenshot code explained)

### ✔ Code:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class base {
public:
    void display() {
        cout << "Display Base\n";
    }

    virtual void show() {
        cout << "Show Base\n";
    }
};

class derived : public base {
public:
    void display() {
        cout << "Display Derived\n";
    }

    void show() {
        cout << "Show Derived\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    base B;
    derived D;

    base *bptr;

    // base pointer → base object
    bptr = &B;
    cout << "bptr points to Base\n";
    bptr->display();  // Base version
    bptr->show();     // Base version

    // base pointer → derived object
    bptr = &D;
    cout << "bptr points to Derived\n";
    bptr->display();  // Base version (non-virtual)
    bptr->show();     // Derived version (virtual)

    return 0;
}
```

### ✔ OUTPUT explained:

```
bptr points to Base
Display Base   ← base version
Show Base      ← base version

bptr points to Derived
Display Base   ← display() is not virtual (static binding)
Show Derived   ← show() is virtual (dynamic binding)
```

✔ Because `show()` is virtual  
✔ So when pointer points to derived → derived version runs.

---

# 🎯 4) Important Note (from your PDF)

> Virtual functions must be accessed using a base class pointer.  
> Using object-name dot operator does NOT create polymorphism.

Example:

```
B obj;
obj.show();  // always calls derived version, no polymorphism involved
```

Polymorphism = ONLY when using **base pointer**.

