# ⭐ 4) **Hybrid Inheritance**
Hybrid = **combination of two or more types**  
Most common:  
→ Multiple + multilevel mixed together

Example Structure:

```
       A
     /   \
    B     C
     \   /
       D
```

### ✔ SIMPLE EXAMPLE

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class A {
public:
    void showA() {
        cout << "Class A\n";
    }
};

class B : public A {   // A → B
public:
    void showB() {
        cout << "Class B\n";
    }
};

class C : public A {   // A → C
public:
    void showC() {
        cout << "Class C\n";
    }
};

class D : public B, public C {  // hybrid
public:
    void showD() {
        cout << "Class D\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    D obj;

    // obj.showA(); ❌  ERROR (diamond problem)

    // to fix diamond problem, we use virtual inheritance
}
```

---

# ⭐ Hybrid With Virtual Inheritance (Correct Version)
To remove duplicates, use `virtual`:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class A {
public:
    void showA() {
        cout << "Class A\n";
    }
};

class B : virtual public A {};
class C : virtual public A {};

class D : public B, public C {
public:
    void showD() {
        cout << "Class D\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    D obj;
    obj.showA();
    obj.showD();
}
```

✔ Now only **one** A is inherited → no conflict.

